---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Card from '../components/ui/Card.astro';
import ArchitectureDiagram from '../components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro';

// Every claim on this page is limited to founder-approved positioning
// (home/pricing copy + locked plan decisions). No overclaiming: fail-closed
// is "designed", never a platform-specific persistence promise.

const cantSee = [
  {
    what: 'Your traffic',
    detail:
      'WireGuard tunnels terminate on your gateways. Packets never route through anything we operate.',
  },
  {
    what: 'Your network keys',
    detail:
      'WireGuard keys are generated and stored on your machines. There is no key escrow — your private keys never touch our systems.',
  },
  {
    what: 'Your configs',
    detail: 'Network topology, ACLs, and policies live in your control plane — self-hosted.',
  },
  {
    what: 'Your users',
    detail:
      'Identity stays between your control plane and your identity provider. We never see accounts or sign-ins.',
  },
  {
    what: 'Your logs',
    detail: 'Audit logs are written and kept inside your infrastructure. Nothing streams to us.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Tunnex — Security"
  description="Tunnex security model: your infrastructure is its own trust domain. Hosted service holds billing and license records only — offline license verification, fail-closed client design."
>
  <section class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl flex-col gap-3 px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 md:py-20">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">Security</h1>
    <p class="max-w-2xl text-lg text-text-muted">
      The honest version: the most secure component is the one that isn’t there. Tunnex is built so
      our infrastructure cannot touch your network — not by policy, by architecture.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl flex-col gap-6 px-4 pb-16 sm:px-6">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Two trust domains. One of them is yours.</h2>
    <p class="max-w-2xl text-text-muted">
      Everything that moves or protects your data — control plane, gateways, clients — runs on
      infrastructure you own. Our hosted service is a separate trust domain that holds exactly two
      kinds of records: billing and licenses. It is never in your traffic path, and your VPN keeps
      running even if tunnex.io goes dark.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <div class="min-w-[600px] sm:min-w-0"><ArchitectureDiagram /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-y border-border bg-surface/50">
    <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl flex-col gap-6 px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">What we cannot see</h2>
      <p class="max-w-2xl text-sm text-text-muted">
        Not “do not look at” — cannot. These never leave your infrastructure:
      </p>
      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-6">
        {
          cantSee.map((item, i) => (
            <div class={i < 3 ? 'lg:col-span-2' : 'lg:col-span-3'}>
              <Card class="h-full">
                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold">{item.what}</h3>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-text-muted">{item.detail}</p>
              </Card>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-10 px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 md:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Licenses verify offline</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
        Enterprise license keys are verified inside your deployment against a public key that ships
        in the binary. There is no phone-home, no license server in your critical path, and
        air-gapped deployments are fully supported.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
        When a license expires: a grace period with warnings, then Enterprise features lapse to the
        Open tier. The VPN keeps running — license state never touches the data plane. Upgrading
        again is pasting a new key, not a reinstall.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Designed to fail closed</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
        The desktop clients include a kill-switch designed to fail closed: if the tunnel drops,
        traffic stops rather than silently falling back to the open network. Fail-open is a design
        bug, not a convenience.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
        We say “designed to” deliberately — hardening this behavior on every platform is ongoing
        engineering, and we would rather under-promise here than oversell a security boundary.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-border bg-surface/50">
    <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl flex-col gap-3 px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Found a vulnerability?</h2>
      <p class="max-w-2xl text-sm text-text-muted">
        Email
        <a href="mailto:security@tunnex.io" class="font-semibold text-link"> security@tunnex.io</a>.
        A human reads every report, and we will acknowledge yours. We don’t run a bug bounty
        program. Please give us a reasonable window to fix the issue before public disclosure.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
